# Vocabulary, state model, and reviewed waivers — applied AFTER corpus.jsonl.
#
# Three groups, in order:
#   1. terms/antonyms: the domain nouns and the one verb opposition the semantic tier surfaced.
#   2. state model: the org plane's transition matrix as a declared enum, so the reachability
#      tier can prove the one-live-run invariant over EVERY reachable state rather than a bound.
#   3. waivers: every remaining finding class, each with the reason it is not a defect.
#
# Rebuild with:
#   symspec apply --ops docs/formal/requirements/vocabulary.jsonl \
#                 --file docs/formal/requirements/requirements.json

# ── 1. Domain vocabulary ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nouns only. `term` refuses a phrase carrying a verb the antonym table or state bridge reads,
# because substituting one would move the polarity the solver computes without moving the parse
# that recognises it. Each canonical spelling is the one src/pneuma/team/ uses.

{"op":"term","canonical":"revision identifier","alias":"revision id"}
{"op":"term","canonical":"revision identifier","alias":"content address"}
{"op":"term","canonical":"conflict row","alias":"conflicts row"}
{"op":"term","canonical":"task contract","alias":"blackboard task"}
{"op":"term","canonical":"design question","alias":"decides field"}
{"op":"term","canonical":"divergence verdict","alias":"split-brain verdict"}
{"op":"term","canonical":"rounds counter","alias":"revise budget"}

# The one opposition the semantic tier proposed (FND_OPPOSITION_CANDIDATE, CON-12 / CON-14).
# `insert the conflict row` and `delete the conflict row` are genuinely polar operations on one
# row, so committing the pair collapses them onto one atom at opposite polarity — which is what
# lets the solver DECIDE whether CON-12 and CON-14 agree instead of leaving them uncompared.
{"op":"antonym","a":"insert","b":"delete"}

# A second opposition, committed on the authoring pass rather than proposed by the tier.
# `keep the rounds counter` and `refill the rounds counter` are the two halves of budget
# monotonicity (core.py:299-302), so committing the pair let the solver DECIDE the relation
# between EXE-4 and EXE-5. It immediately proved FND_REDUNDANCY: as first written, EXE-4
# ("keep the counter when the walk restarts") was bi-implied by EXE-5 ("shall not refill the
# counter when the walk restarts") — one claim written twice. EXE-4 was re-pointed at the
# MECHANISM (the counter is created once, outside the loop) and the redundancy cleared. Kept
# committed because it is what makes any future rewording of either side provably comparable.
{"op":"antonym","a":"keep","b":"refill"}

# ── 2. The org plane's state model ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The transition matrix from docs/design/org-plane.md:14, 92-97 plus the matrix supplied with
# the task. `frame: stable` on both variables is declared deliberately: the honest cost is that
# any proof arrives as PROVED_UNDER_HYPOTHESES naming them, and that demotion is the truthful
# reading — no requirement in this corpus states that nothing else writes these variables.

{"op":"state","name":"task_state","type":"enum","domain":["DRAFT","READY","ASSIGNED","RUNNING","SUBMITTED","UNDER_REVIEW","ACCEPTED","REVISION_REQUIRED","BLOCKED","FAILED","CANCELLED","SUPERSEDED"],"frame":"stable","initial":"task_state = DRAFT"}
{"op":"state","name":"live_runs","type":"int","min":0,"max":4,"frame":"stable","initial":"live_runs = 0"}

{"op":"classify","ref":"ORG-1","kind":"effect","expression":"when task_state = DRAFT: task_state := READY"}
{"op":"classify","ref":"ORG-2","kind":"effect","expression":"when task_state = READY: task_state := ASSIGNED"}
{"op":"classify","ref":"ORG-3","kind":"effect","expression":"when task_state = ASSIGNED: task_state := RUNNING, live_runs := live_runs + 1"}
{"op":"classify","ref":"ORG-4","kind":"effect","expression":"when task_state = RUNNING: task_state := SUBMITTED, live_runs := live_runs - 1"}
{"op":"classify","ref":"ORG-5","kind":"effect","expression":"when task_state = SUBMITTED: task_state := UNDER_REVIEW"}
{"op":"classify","ref":"ORG-6","kind":"effect","expression":"when task_state = UNDER_REVIEW: task_state := ACCEPTED"}
{"op":"classify","ref":"ORG-7","kind":"effect","expression":"when task_state = UNDER_REVIEW: task_state := REVISION_REQUIRED"}
{"op":"classify","ref":"ORG-8","kind":"effect","expression":"when task_state = REVISION_REQUIRED: task_state := READY"}
{"op":"classify","ref":"ORG-9","kind":"effect","expression":"when task_state = BLOCKED: task_state := READY"}
{"op":"classify","ref":"ORG-11","kind":"constraint","expression":"live_runs <= 1"}

# ── 3. Reviewed waivers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each carries the reason the finding is not a defect in THIS corpus. A waiver without a reason
# is indistinguishable from neglect, which is why the reason is required.

{"op":"waive","code":"GTWR_R24_PRONOUN","reason":"Every remaining hit is the CARDINAL DETERMINER 'one' ('one hook', 'one revision row', 'one live run'), not a pronoun with an unclear referent. GTWR_R32 asks for 'each' over 'all/any/both' and this corpus obeys it; 'one' is the singular counterpart and its referent is the noun it modifies. The four genuine pronoun hits ('it', 'that') were rewritten rather than waived."}
{"op":"waive","code":"GTWR_R35_TEMPORAL","reason":"Two classes, neither indefinite. (a) 'as' in 'record X as Y' / 'return X as text' is the copular preposition, not a temporal keyword — the lexical scan cannot tell them apart. (b) 'before' / 'after' / 'latest' in EXE-6, EXE-7, EXE-8, EXE-9, EXE-19, EXE-26 and EXE-29 name a DEFINITE ordering that IS the requirement: worklog.py:126-133 appends before it awaits, and core.py:325 reads the cap off the latest verdict. R35 targets 'eventually'/'until'; no requirement here uses either."}
{"op":"waive","code":"GTWR_R19_COMBINATOR","reason":"Each hit is a single compound OBJECT, not two thoughts: 'the document text and the lineage together' is one digest input tuple (artifacts.py:435-450), 'each member and the lead' is one retire gather (core.py:277-281), the three identifiers in XPL-9 are one composite join key (org-plane.md:106-109), and 'one run or fewer' is one numeric bound. Splitting any of them would state something the code does not: the digest over content alone is a different function."}
{"op":"waive","code":"GTWR_R15_LOGICAL_EXPR","reason":"The lowercase 'and' sits in a PRECONDITION or TRIGGER slot where it conjoins genuinely separate guard conditions — ORG-14's release gate really is accepted-reviews AND zero-unresolved-conflicts AND divergence-not-confirmed (org-plane.md:101-105). symspec's own atomizer reads the slot text as prose, so adopting the bracketed [X AND Y] convention would change what the solver compares without changing what the gate means."}
{"op":"waive","code":"GTWR_R5_INDEFINITE_ARTICLE","reason":"Both hits are correctly indefinite. EXE-23 refuses ANY in-memory database, not a particular one (trajectory.py:161 tests for two substrings). XPL-7 quantifies over any revision that carries a decides value (artifacts.py:182-185)."}
{"op":"waive","code":"FND_LEAF_UNVERIFIABLE","reason":"Every refinement leaf here carries verificationMethod plus a verificationNote naming the exact source file and line range, or the design-doc section, it was read off — which IS the independent verification obligation KAOS leaf-verifiability asks for. Adding a `verifies` EDGE would mean inventing verification requirements that restate the notes, growing the corpus without adding a claim."}
{"op":"waive","code":"FND_SIMILAR_SEMANTIC","reason":"Reviewed and DECLINED, class by class. The largest cluster is the nine org-plane transitions, which are deliberately distinct rows of one transition matrix — collapsing 'move to assigned' and 'move to running' onto one atom would make the matrix unprovable and would manufacture conflicts between legal transitions. The content-plane cluster ('advance main to the proposal revision' / 'to the seeded revision') names two genuinely different target revisions; merging them would prove a conflict the plane does not contain."}
{"op":"waive","code":"FND_SIMILAR_UNUNIFIED","reason":"Same subject as the semantic-tier waiver, reached lexically instead: the org-plane transitions share almost every word because they share a shape, and CON-21/CON-22 differ only in which party is denied which tool — which is exactly the asymmetry the two requirements exist to state (docs/design/artifacts.md:61-84)."}
{"op":"waive","code":"FND_MISSING_TRACE_LINK","reason":"Propose-only trace candidates between requirements that are near-neighbours in wording but not in refinement. The refinement DAG this corpus commits follows the CODE's own containment (a guard's special case refines its general rule); a cosine-suggested edge between two sibling transitions would assert a derivation the source does not have."}
{"op":"waive","code":"FND_DUPLICATE_CLUSTER","reason":"Four clusters, each a deliberate family rather than a near-duplication: the nine org transitions, the three teardown-ordering guarantees (core.py:263-283), the six content-plane write rules, and the three merge-refusal rules. Each member states a different guard over a shared subject, which is what a transition matrix and a write model look like when written one rule at a time."}
{"op":"waive","code":"GTWR_R16_NEGATION","reason":"All 25 hits are the `shall not` rendering of an unwanted-behavior requirement authored with --negated, which is symspec's OWN mechanism for putting a prohibition and its permission on one atom at opposite polarity so the solver can prove a conflict. R16 asks for positive phrasing; obeying it here would put the prohibition on a second atom and silently destroy the contradiction detection this corpus exists to run — including the two proven findings. The polarity is encoded structurally, not smuggled into the response text."}
{"op":"waive","code":"FND_TERM_INCONSISTENT","reason":"One pair, ORG-10 and ORG-11, flagged because the committed term 'task contract' spans two contexts sitting at cosine 0.59. Read against the source they are the SAME object: ORG-10 forbids an out-of-matrix transition of a task contract and ORG-11 bounds the live runs of a task contract, both properties of the kernel's one task-contract row (docs/design/org-plane.md:14, 92-97). The contexts are lexically distant because one is about legality and the other about concurrency, which is two properties of one thing rather than one word for two things."}
{"op":"waive","code":"FND_AMBIGUITY_NEEDS_JUDGMENT","reason":"Assessed by hand against the source. Every flagged phrase resolves to one named code object: 'the run body' is core.py:233-262's try block, 'the tool body' is the strands_tool closure in hooks/artifacts.py, 'the closed vocabulary' is worklog.py:50's DISCOVERY_KINDS, and 'the transition matrix' is the kernel's own matrix (org-plane.md:14). The verificationNote on each requirement carries the file and line range that disambiguates it."}
